  "Question": "The value(s) of \\(y\\) for which the following pair of equations \\[ \\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2-16&=0,\\\\ x^2-3y+12&=0 \\end{aligned} \\] may have a real common solution, are:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\) only",
    "B": "\\(-7,\\) \\(4\\)",
    "C": "\\(0,\\) \\(4\\)",
    "D": "no \\(y\\)",
    "E": "all \\(y\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Both equations contain \\(x^2\\), so express that same quantity in two ways. Because \\(x\\) is real, its square must be nonnegative:\n\\[\nx^2=16-y^2=3y-12\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe condition \\(3y-12\\ge0\\) gives \\(y\\ge4\\). Meanwhile, \\(16-y^2\\ge0\\) gives \\(y^2\\le16\\), so \\(-4\\le y\\le4\\). The only value meeting both bounds is \\(y=4\\).\n\nFor \\(y=4\\), either equation gives \\(x^2=0\\), and \\(x=0\\) satisfies both original equations. Therefore \\(y=4\\) only, choice A.",
